================================================================================================================================
OVERVIEW
========

Feature #27 — Server-Side Validation Mirror is COMPLETE.

The purpose of this feature was to establish a server-side validation layer for manual execution data before it reaches the atomic manual execution persistence layer.

The key architectural goal was:

CLIENT VALIDATION
↓
SERVER-SIDE VALIDATION
↓
ATOMIC PERSISTENCE
↓
SUPABASE EXECUTIONS

Client-side validation remains useful for UX, but it is NOT treated as a security or data-integrity boundary.

The server must independently validate manual execution data because a client can be bypassed or manipulated.

This feature was implemented as an addition to the existing atomic manual trade persistence architecture.

No changes were made to:

* FIFO reconstruction behavior
* pairTrades.ts
* reconstructed trades
* P&L calculations
* analytics
* Dashboard V2
* broker sync behavior
* broker execution replacement logic
* manual trade UI behavior
* canonical execution architecture

The canonical architecture remains:

Broker / Manual Entry
↓
Normalized Executions
↓
Supabase Executions Ledger
↓
Deterministic FIFO Reconstruction
↓
Trades / Analytics / Dashboard

================================================================================================================================
WHY FEATURE #27 WAS REQUIRED
============================

Before this feature, the atomic manual execution replacement layer protected persistence through a database transaction, but the server did not have a dedicated validation mirror for the normalized manual execution objects.

The client-side Manual Entry / Edit UI already performed validation.

However:

CLIENT VALIDATION ≠ SERVER VALIDATION

A malicious or malformed request could bypass the UI and call the server endpoint directly.

Therefore the server needed to independently verify that incoming manual executions satisfy the basic invariants expected by the canonical execution model.

The purpose of #27 was NOT to redesign the entire execution schema.

The purpose was to create a focused server-side validation boundary for manual executions.

